Expanded FAQs
RBAC (Role-Based Access Control): Manifests specify every action’s allowed roles (e.g.,
AUDIT_MANAGER, FRAUD_ANALYST), enabling fine-grained, team/org-wide permissioning.
ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control): Each workflow can enforce row-level, attributederived rules based on context (dept, site, business unit, risk_category, etc.), supporting
even more granular restrictions.
These controls are not duplicated per use case: they’re managed in central Security policies
where each action_id gets its own RBAC/ABAC block.
If a use case requires fundamentally different governance, security, or compliance, a new
action_id with distinct RBAC/ABAC rules is created (e.g., international audits having stricter
entitlements).
Every event (button click, API call, action execution, approval, etc.) is logged with full
timestamps and user-context.
This event data serves as training/analytics data for productivity analysis: measuring
analyst time-to-resolution, workflow bottlenecks, manual vs. automated effort, and user
adoption.
Management can use dashboards to track who is most/least productive, which processes
are most/least efficient, and where to deploy further automation for ROI improvements.
